/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ccnp-encor.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ccnp-encor", [["en1",0,1,"A mid-size campus has a single building with 12 access switches and no plans to grow. The team wants to cut cost and hop count while keeping redundancy. Which design fits best?",["A two-tier collapsed core with one redundant switch pair","A three-tier design with separate access, distribution and core layers","A spine-leaf fabric with every access switch acting as a spine","A flat Layer 2 network with one large VLAN spanning every switch"],0,"A collapsed core suits a small, single-building campus: it keeps redundancy while removing a layer. A separate core pays off when many distribution blocks must be interconnected.","Exam topics section 1.0 (enterprise network design)"],["en2",0,1,"In a data center spine-leaf (Clos) fabric, how are the switches connected?",["Every spine connects to every other spine, and each leaf connects to one spine","Leaves connect in a ring, and spines connect only to the WAN routers","Every leaf links to every spine, and leaves never link to each other","Each leaf connects to two other leaves, and one spine connects to the core"],2,"In a Clos fabric each leaf uplinks to every spine, so any two leaves are always the same number of hops apart. Spines do not interconnect with each other.","Exam topics section 1.0 (enterprise network design)"],["en3",0,1,"A pair of Catalyst 9500 switches runs Stateful Switchover (SSO) with dual supervisors. What does SSO provide when the active supervisor fails?",["Both supervisors forward traffic at once, doubling the switch throughput","The standby supervisor takes over with synced state, so forwarding continues","The switch reboots faster because the image is cached in flash","The neighboring routers take over the gateway role for the VLANs through HSRP"],1,"SSO keeps the standby supervisor synchronized so it can take over without resetting line cards. It is not active-active forwarding, and it is separate from FHRP gateway redundancy.","Exam topics section 1.0 (high availability)"],["en4",0,1,"Which statement correctly contrasts the SD-WAN control plane with its data plane?",["The WAN Edge routers exchange routes over OMP, while the Controllers forward user traffic between sites","The Manager forwards user traffic, while the Validator pushes templates to every edge","The Controllers forward traffic over IPsec, while the Manager exchanges routes over BGP","Controllers exchange routes and policy over OMP; WAN Edges carry user traffic in IPsec tunnels"],3,"In Cisco Catalyst SD-WAN, the Controllers (formerly vSmart) run OMP to distribute routes and policy; the WAN Edges build IPsec tunnels and carry the user traffic. The Manager handles management and the Validator handles orchestration and onboarding.","Exam topics section 1.0 (SD-WAN)"],["en5",0,1,"A new SD-WAN edge router powers up at a branch. Which component first authenticates it and tells it where to find the Controllers and Manager?",["The SD-WAN Manager (formerly vManage)","The SD-WAN Controller (formerly vSmart)","The Catalyst Center assurance engine","The SD-WAN Validator (formerly vBond)"],3,"The Validator is the orchestration plane: it authenticates devices joining the fabric and points them to the Controllers and Manager. The Manager is the management GUI, and the Controllers handle routing and policy.","Exam topics section 1.0 (SD-WAN)"],["en6",0,1,"In Cisco SD-Access, which protocol forms the fabric control plane that tracks where each endpoint is located?",["VXLAN, with the border node flooding endpoint locations to every edge","LISP, with the control plane node as map server and map resolver","OSPF, with each edge node advertising every host route as an external LSA","CTS, with scalable group tags carrying each endpoint's location"],1,"SD-Access uses LISP for the control plane, VXLAN for the data plane and Cisco TrustSec (SGTs) for policy. The control plane node is the LISP map server and resolver.","Exam topics section 1.0 (SD-Access)"],["en7",0,1,"What does VXLAN provide in an SD-Access fabric?",["The data plane encapsulation that carries traffic across the routed underlay","The control plane database that maps endpoint identifiers to locators","The policy plane that tags traffic with scalable group identifiers at the edge","The management plane that pushes configuration from Catalyst Center"],0,"VXLAN is the SD-Access data plane: it encapsulates frames in UDP across the underlay. LISP is the control plane and TrustSec is the policy plane.","Exam topics section 1.0 (SD-Access)"],["en8",0,1,"Compared with a traditional campus, which is a key advantage of SD-Access?",["It removes the need for any underlay routing protocol between the switches","It lets every access switch keep its own manually written ACL set","Policy follows users by group identity, not VLAN or IP, and is pushed centrally","It extends one Layer 2 VLAN across the whole campus using spanning tree between buildings"],2,"SD-Access applies group-based policy (SGTs) centrally through Catalyst Center, independent of IP addressing. It still needs a routed underlay and avoids campus-wide spanning tree.","Exam topics section 1.0 (SD-Access)"],["en9",0,1,"Voice packets arrive at a WAN router already marked DSCP EF. The WAN link is congested. Which QoS tool gives the voice traffic strict priority when it is sent out the interface?",["Weighted random early detection on the voice class","Low latency queuing (LLQ) with a priority queue for EF traffic","Class-based policing that drops excess voice packets","Traffic shaping on the ingress interface of the router"],1,"LLQ adds a strict-priority queue to CBWFQ so delay-sensitive EF traffic is sent first. WRED drops packets early to avoid TCP synchronization and is not meant for voice.","Exam topics section 1.0 (QoS)"],["en10",0,1,"A service provider sells a 50 Mb/s service on a 1 Gb/s handoff and drops anything above the contract. What should the customer configure on its egress interface to avoid those drops?",["Policing to 50 Mb/s, which drops or remarks bursts immediately","Marking all traffic as DSCP CS1 so the provider treats it as scavenger","WRED on the provider's ingress interface to smooth the traffic","Shaping to 50 Mb/s, which buffers bursts and sends them later"],3,"Shaping queues excess traffic and releases it at the contracted rate, so the provider's policer does not drop it. Policing on the customer side would still drop the bursts.","Exam topics section 1.0 (QoS)"],["en11",0,1,"Which table does Cisco Express Forwarding use to rewrite the Layer 2 header of a packet it is about to forward?",["The RIB, which holds every route learned from the routing protocols","The CAM table, which maps VLAN and MAC address pairs to ports","The adjacency table, which holds next-hop Layer 2 rewrite data","The TCAM, which holds the ACL and QoS match entries"],2,"CEF combines the FIB (derived from the RIB) for the next-hop lookup with the adjacency table for the Layer 2 rewrite. The CAM table is for Layer 2 switching.","Exam topics section 1.0 (hardware and software switching)"],["en12",0,1,"Why do Catalyst switches store ACL and QoS entries in TCAM rather than in a standard CAM table?",["TCAM matches on three states (0, 1, don't care), so it can look up masked entries like prefixes in one step","TCAM stores only exact 48-bit MAC matches, which makes lookups faster","TCAM is software memory, so it can hold unlimited entries","TCAM is only used when a packet is punted to the route processor"],0,"Ternary CAM supports a don't-care bit, so it can match prefixes and ACL masks in hardware. Binary CAM only does exact matches, such as MAC lookups.","Exam topics section 1.0 (hardware and software switching)"],["en13",0,1,"A router shows high CPU, and show ip cef indicates many packets are being punted. What is the most likely consequence?",["They are process switched by the CPU instead of by CEF or hardware","Those packets are dropped at ingress because CEF is disabled","Those packets are switched faster because they skip the FIB lookup","Those packets are sent to the standby supervisor for forwarding"],0,"Punted packets go to the route processor for process switching, which is slow and loads the CPU. CEF-switched traffic avoids the CPU.","Exam topics section 1.0 (hardware and software switching)"],["en14",0,1,"Which DSCP value is the standard marking for interactive voice bearer traffic?",["AF41 (34)","CS3 (24)","EF (46)","AF11 (10)"],2,"EF (46) is the conventional marking for voice bearer traffic. AF41 is commonly used for interactive video and CS3 for call signaling.","Exam topics section 1.0 (QoS)"],["en15",0,2,"A team needs to run 20 Linux servers on a host with no general-purpose OS underneath, managed by vCenter. Which platform type is this?",["A type 2 hypervisor such as VirtualBox","A type 1 hypervisor such as VMware ESXi","A container runtime such as Docker Engine","A Kubernetes worker node running pods"],1,"ESXi is a bare-metal type 1 hypervisor. VirtualBox is type 2 because it runs on a host OS, and containers share the host kernel instead of running full guest operating systems.","Exam topics section 2.0 (device virtualization)"],["en16",0,2,"What is the main job of a virtual switch such as the vSphere standard switch?",["Route traffic between VRFs using a separate routing table per VM","Encrypt all traffic between VMs on the same host with IPsec","Run spanning tree with the physical switches to block redundant uplinks","Connect VM vNICs to each other and to the host's physical uplinks"],3,"A virtual switch forwards frames between vNICs and physical uplinks and can tag VLANs. It does not participate in spanning tree the way a physical switch does.","Exam topics section 2.0 (device virtualization)"],["en17",0,2,"Two customers connected to the same branch router both use 10.1.1.0/24. They must stay isolated without buying another router. What should the engineer configure?",["Two OSPF processes that share the global routing table","A single VLAN with private VLAN isolation on the router","Policy-based routing that matches each customer's source subnet to a next hop","VRF-Lite, giving each customer's interfaces a separate routing table"],3,"VRF-Lite gives each customer an independent routing table, so overlapping subnets can coexist. Separate OSPF processes still install into the global table, where the prefixes would collide.","Exam topics section 2.0 (VRF)"],["en18",0,2,"An engineer configures interface GigabitEthernet0/1 with vrf forwarding CUST-A after the IP address was already set. What happens?",["The IP address moves into the VRF automatically with no change","The IP address is removed and must be entered again","The interface stays in the global table until the router reloads","The command is rejected until the interface is shut down"],1,"Assigning an interface to a VRF clears its IP configuration, so the address must be reapplied. This is a common mistake in VRF labs.","Exam topics section 2.0 (VRF)"],["en19",0,2,"A GRE tunnel keeps going up and down, and the log shows \"recursive routing\" messages. What is the most likely cause?",["The route to the tunnel destination is learned through the tunnel itself","The tunnel source and destination use different MTU sizes","The tunnel keepalive timer is shorter than the OSPF hello timer on the link","IPsec is not configured, so GRE refuses to stay up"],0,"Recursive routing occurs when the best path to the tunnel destination points into the tunnel. Fix it by making sure the underlay route to the destination is preferred and not advertised over the tunnel.","Exam topics section 2.0 (GRE and IPsec tunneling)"],["en20",0,2,"Why is GRE often combined with IPsec for site-to-site VPNs across the internet?",["GRE adds encryption, and IPsec adds support for multicast routing updates","IPsec alone cannot carry any unicast IP traffic between sites","GRE carries multicast and routing protocol traffic, and IPsec adds encryption and integrity","GRE removes the need for IKE by exchanging keys itself"],2,"Plain IPsec (crypto map) does not carry multicast, so OSPF or EIGRP cannot run over it. GRE provides the multicast-capable tunnel and IPsec protects it.","Exam topics section 2.0 (GRE and IPsec tunneling)"],["en21",0,2,"Which IPsec mode encrypts the entire original IP packet and adds a new IP header?",["Transport mode","Tunnel mode","Aggressive mode","Main mode"],1,"Tunnel mode wraps the whole original packet in a new IP header. Transport mode protects only the payload. Main and aggressive are IKEv1 phase 1 modes, not IPsec encapsulation modes.","Exam topics section 2.0 (GRE and IPsec tunneling)"],["en22",0,2,"In LISP, what is the difference between an EID and an RLOC?",["An EID is the router's loopback address, and an RLOC is the endpoint's MAC address","An EID is used only in the underlay, and an RLOC is used only in the overlay network","An EID is a VXLAN network ID, and an RLOC is a VRF route distinguisher","An EID identifies the endpoint; an RLOC is the router address where it is reached"],3,"LISP separates identity (EID) from location (RLOC). The map server stores EID-to-RLOC mappings, and tunnel routers encapsulate traffic toward the RLOC.","Exam topics section 2.0 (LISP and VXLAN)"],["en23",0,2,"How many Layer 2 segments can VXLAN identify, and why does that matter?",["About 4,000, because the VNI is 12 bits, the same as the VLAN ID","About 65,000, because the VNI reuses the 16-bit UDP destination port field","About 16 million, because the 24-bit VNI overcomes the 4094-VLAN limit","About 1 million, because the VNI is 20 bits like an MPLS label"],2,"The VXLAN Network Identifier is 24 bits, giving roughly 16 million segments versus 4094 usable 802.1Q VLANs. VXLAN travels in UDP (port 4789).","Exam topics section 2.0 (LISP and VXLAN)"],["en24",0,3,"SW1 Gi1/0/1 is set to switchport mode dynamic auto, and SW2 Gi1/0/1 is also dynamic auto. What is the result?",["It stays an access port, because neither side initiates a trunk","The link becomes a trunk, because both sides support DTP","The link goes err-disabled, because the DTP modes conflict with each other","The link becomes a trunk with native VLAN 1 only"],0,"Dynamic auto only responds to DTP requests; it never initiates. Two auto ports never negotiate a trunk. Desirable-to-auto or trunk-to-auto would form one.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: trunking)"],["en25",0,3,"A trunk passes traffic for most VLANs, but hosts in VLAN 30 cannot reach each other across it. show interfaces trunk lists VLAN 30 as active on both switches. What should be checked next?",["The allowed VLAN list on each side of the trunk","The DTP mode on the access ports in VLAN 30","The VTP domain name on the distribution switch","The EtherChannel load-balancing hash"],0,"If a VLAN exists and is active but not in the allowed list on one side, frames for it are not carried. show interfaces trunk shows allowed and forwarding VLANs separately.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: trunking)"],["en26",0,3,"Two switches are joined by four links. SW1 ports use channel-group 1 mode active and SW2 ports use channel-group 1 mode passive. What happens?",["No port channel forms, because both sides must be set to LACP active","A PAgP port channel forms, because passive is a PAgP mode","LACP forms the port channel: active initiates and passive responds","The links go err-disabled because of an EtherChannel misconfiguration"],2,"LACP active sends LACPDUs and passive responds, so active/passive works. Passive/passive fails because neither initiates. PAgP uses desirable and auto.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: EtherChannel)"],["en27",0,3,"An EtherChannel shows one member port as suspended (s) in show etherchannel summary. What is the most likely cause?",["The port is the only one carrying traffic because of the hash algorithm","Its speed, duplex or VLAN settings do not match the other members","The port has more bandwidth than the others and was promoted to primary","The port channel interface has been shut down administratively"],1,"Members must match in speed, duplex, mode and VLAN settings; a mismatched port is suspended. A shut port channel would show all members down, not one suspended.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: EtherChannel)"],["en28",0,3,"All traffic between two servers uses only one of the four links in an EtherChannel. Why?",["EtherChannel sends each packet round-robin but the other links are in standby","LACP limits a single port channel to one active link at a time","The port channel is misconfigured, because EtherChannel always splits every flow","The load-balancing hash picks one link per flow, and every packet in this flow hashes to the same link"],3,"EtherChannel hashes on addresses or ports so a given flow always uses the same member, which prevents reordering. Changing the hash to include Layer 4 ports can spread flows better.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: EtherChannel)"],["en29",0,3,"In Rapid PVST+, which port role provides an immediate backup path to the root bridge if the root port fails?",["The backup port","The designated port","The edge port","The alternate port"],3,"An alternate port is a discarding port with an alternate path to the root, and it becomes the root port right away if the root port fails. A backup port backs up a designated port on a shared segment.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: RSTP)"],["en30",0,3,"A campus has 200 VLANs and only two distinct forwarding topologies. The team wants to reduce the number of spanning-tree instances. Which protocol should they use?",["Rapid PVST+, with one instance per VLAN","MST, mapping the VLANs into two instances","PVST+, with PortFast on every trunk","STP 802.1D, with a single common spanning tree"],1,"MST maps many VLANs to a few instances, cutting CPU and BPDU load while still allowing load sharing. 802.1D has one tree for everything and cannot load-balance VLANs.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: MST)"],["en31",0,3,"Two MST switches share a link, but they behave as if they are in different regions. Which setting must match for them to be in the same region?",["The region name, revision number and VLAN-to-instance map","The bridge priority for instance 0 on both switches","The native VLAN and the allowed VLAN list on the trunk between them","The VTP domain name and VTP version"],0,"An MST region is defined by name, revision and the VLAN-to-instance table; any difference puts the switches in separate regions. Bridge priority affects root election, not region membership.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: MST)"],["en32",0,3,"An access port has PortFast and BPDU guard enabled. A user plugs a small switch into it, and the port goes err-disabled. Why?",["PortFast shut the port because the switch sent more than one MAC address","Root guard shut the port because a superior BPDU arrived","BPDU guard shut the port because it received a BPDU on an edge port","Loop guard shut the port because BPDUs stopped arriving"],2,"BPDU guard err-disables an edge port that receives any BPDU. Root guard puts a port in root-inconsistent state instead, and only on superior BPDUs.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: STP features)"],["en33",0,3,"Which feature prevents a distribution switch's downlink from becoming a root port if a newly connected switch advertises a better bridge ID?",["BPDU filter","Root guard","PortFast","UplinkFast"],1,"Root guard blocks a port (root-inconsistent) when it receives a superior BPDU, protecting the planned root placement. BPDU filter just stops sending and receiving BPDUs, which can cause loops.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: STP features)"],["en34",0,3,"Which statement correctly contrasts EIGRP and OSPF?",["EIGRP is link-state using SPF, while OSPF is distance-vector using DUAL","Both are link-state, but only OSPF supports unequal-cost load balancing","Both use DUAL, but only EIGRP requires a backbone area","EIGRP is an advanced distance-vector protocol using DUAL, while OSPF is link-state using SPF"],3,"EIGRP uses DUAL and feasible successors; OSPF floods LSAs and runs SPF. EIGRP (not OSPF) supports unequal-cost load balancing with variance, and OSPF (not EIGRP) needs area 0.","Exam topics section 3.0 (EIGRP vs OSPF)"],["en35",0,3,"R1 has two EIGRP paths to 10.5.0.0/16: feasible distance 2000 via R2 and 3000 via R3. R3's reported distance is 1500. The engineer sets variance 2. What happens?",["Only the R2 path is installed, because variance applies only to OSPF","Only the R3 path is installed, because it has the lower reported distance","Both paths are installed, because the R3 path is a feasible successor and within twice the best metric","Both paths are installed with equal traffic, because variance ignores metrics"],2,"The R3 path qualifies as a feasible successor (RD 1500 < FD 2000) and its metric 3000 is within 2 × 2000, so EIGRP installs it and shares load in proportion to the metrics.","Exam topics section 3.0 (EIGRP)"],["en36",0,3,"Which EIGRP feature stops the hub from querying spoke routers when it loses a route, so a hub-and-spoke network converges faster?",["Stub routing on the spoke routers","Auto-summary on the hub router","Split horizon on the spoke routers","Variance on the hub router"],0,"EIGRP stub spokes are not queried by the hub when a route is lost, which limits the query scope and avoids stuck-in-active. Split horizon is a loop-prevention rule, not a query limiter.","Exam topics section 3.0 (EIGRP)"],["en37",0,3,"R1 and R2 are connected on 10.0.12.0/30 but stay in EXSTART/EXCHANGE instead of reaching FULL. Hellos, area and timers match. What is the most likely cause?",["An interface MTU mismatch between the two routers","Mismatched OSPF router IDs on the two routers","A mismatched OSPF process ID on the two routers","Mismatched reference bandwidth on the two routers"],0,"Neighbors stuck in EXSTART/EXCHANGE point to an MTU mismatch, because DBD packets are rejected. Process IDs are local and do not have to match.","Exam topics section 3.0 (OSPF adjacency)"],["en38",0,3,"Which OSPF area type blocks Type 5 LSAs but still allows an ASBR inside the area, using Type 7 LSAs?",["Stub area","Totally stubby area","Not-so-stubby area (NSSA)","Backbone area"],2,"An NSSA lets an internal ASBR originate external routes as Type 7 LSAs, which the ABR translates to Type 5. Stub and totally stubby areas do not allow an ASBR.","Exam topics section 3.0 (OSPF area types)"],["en39",0,3,"An ABR connects area 0 and area 10. The engineer wants routers in area 10 to receive only a default route instead of all inter-area and external routes. What should be configured?",["area 10 nssa on the ABR only, and nothing on the area 10 routers","area 10 stub no-summary on the ABR, and area 10 stub on the area 10 routers","area 10 range on the area 10 internal routers","summary-address 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 on the ABR"],1,"A totally stubby area blocks Type 3, 4 and 5 LSAs except a default route. The no-summary keyword is needed only on the ABR, but every router in the area needs the stub flag.","Exam topics section 3.0 (OSPF area types)"],["en40",0,3,"Two routers are connected point-to-point over Ethernet, and the team wants to skip DR/BDR election to speed up adjacency. What should they configure?",["ip ospf priority 255 on both interfaces","ip ospf cost 1 on both interfaces","passive-interface on both interfaces under the OSPF process","ip ospf network point-to-point on both interfaces"],3,"The point-to-point network type removes DR/BDR election and the wait timer. Priority only influences the election, and passive-interface would stop the adjacency entirely.","Exam topics section 3.0 (OSPF network types)"],["en41",0,3,"How does OSPFv3 differ from OSPFv2 in how neighbors communicate?",["OSPFv3 uses TCP port 179 to exchange LSAs with neighbors","OSPFv3 requires a DR on every link type, including point-to-point links","OSPFv3 elects neighbors by IPv4 address and cannot use a router ID","It runs per link and uses IPv6 link-local addresses as packet sources"],3,"OSPFv3 is enabled on interfaces and uses link-local addresses for adjacencies. It still needs a 32-bit router ID, which must be set manually if no IPv4 address exists.","Exam topics section 3.0 (OSPFv3)"],["en42",0,3,"R1 (AS 65001) and R2 (AS 65002) are directly connected. R1 shows the neighbor in Active state. What does this mean?",["The BGP session is fully up and exchanging prefixes","R1 is trying to open the TCP session, but it is not established yet","R1 has received prefixes from the neighbor but has not installed them","The neighbor has been shut down administratively"],1,"Active means BGP is repeatedly trying to establish the TCP session (port 179), often because of a wrong neighbor IP, AS number or reachability. Established is the working state.","Exam topics section 3.0 (eBGP)"],["en43",0,3,"Which BGP attribute is compared first in Cisco's best-path selection?",["Weight, with the highest value preferred","AS path length, with the shortest preferred","MED, with the lowest value preferred","Local preference, with the lowest value preferred"],0,"Cisco checks weight (local to the router, highest wins) first, then local preference (highest wins), then locally originated, then AS path length, origin and MED.","Exam topics section 3.0 (eBGP best path)"],["en44",0,3,"An enterprise has two ISP links on different routers and wants all outbound traffic to prefer ISP A. Both routers run iBGP. Which attribute should be set on routes received from ISP A?",["A higher weight, which is shared with iBGP peers","A lower MED, which ISP A reads to choose the entry point","A higher local preference, which iBGP peers share","A longer AS path using prepending toward ISP A"],2,"Local preference is carried inside the AS and influences outbound path choice for all routers. Weight is local to one router, and MED and prepending influence inbound traffic.","Exam topics section 3.0 (eBGP best path)"],["en45",0,3,"A branch wants traffic from the guest subnet 10.9.0.0/24 to use a cheaper internet link, while all other traffic follows the routing table. What should be configured?",["A static default route with a higher administrative distance","Policy-based routing, applied inbound on the guest-facing interface","A route map applied outbound on the WAN interface to set the next hop","OSPF cost changes on the guest-facing interface"],1,"PBR matches traffic (with an ACL in a route map) and sets the next hop, overriding the routing table for that traffic. It is applied with ip policy route-map on the ingress interface.","Exam topics section 3.0 (policy-based routing)"],["en46",0,3,"Which statement about VRRP is correct compared with HSRP?",["VRRP is Cisco proprietary and requires a virtual IP that is not on any interface","VRRP disables preemption by default, unlike HSRP","VRRP uses a standby router role instead of a backup role","VRRP is an open standard and can use a real interface address as the virtual IP"],3,"VRRP (RFC 5798) is open, preempts by default, and allows the virtual IP to be the master's own address. HSRP is Cisco proprietary and uses active and standby roles.","Exam topics section 3.0 (FHRP)"],["en47",0,3,"Many inside hosts must reach the internet using one public address, 203.0.113.10, on interface Gi0/0. Which NAT configuration fits?",["Static NAT mapping each host to 203.0.113.10","Dynamic NAT with a pool that contains only 203.0.113.10 and no overload","PAT, using ip nat inside source list with the overload keyword","NAT64 translating the inside hosts to IPv6 addresses"],2,"PAT (NAT overload) lets many hosts share one address by tracking source ports. Dynamic NAT without overload allows only one inside host per public address at a time.","Exam topics section 3.0 (NAT/PAT)"],["en48",0,3,"An engineer checks NTP on a router and sees it synchronized to a server that is stratum 2. What stratum does the router report?",["Stratum 3","Stratum 1","Stratum 2","Stratum 16"],0,"A client is one stratum higher than its source, so it reports stratum 3. Stratum 16 means unsynchronized.","Exam topics section 3.0 (NTP and PTP)"],["en49",0,3,"Which application is best served by Precision Time Protocol (PTP) rather than NTP?",["Industrial and audio-video systems that need sub-microsecond clock accuracy","Setting log timestamps on routers that tolerate a few milliseconds of drift","Keeping user laptops in sync with an internet time server","Validating certificate lifetimes on web servers"],0,"PTP (IEEE 1588) uses hardware timestamping to reach sub-microsecond accuracy. NTP's millisecond accuracy is fine for logs, laptops and certificates.","Exam topics section 3.0 (NTP and PTP)"],["en50",0,3,"A receiver on a switch port wants to join the multicast group 239.1.1.1. Which protocol does the host use to signal this to its local router?",["PIM","MSDP","IGMP","IGMP snooping"],2,"Hosts send IGMP membership reports to the local router. PIM runs between routers, and IGMP snooping is a switch feature that listens to IGMP to limit flooding.","Exam topics section 3.0 (multicast)"],["en51",0,3,"In PIM sparse mode, what is the role of the rendezvous point (RP)?",["It floods multicast to every interface and prunes where there are no receivers","It is the shared-tree root where sources register and receivers first join","It assigns multicast group addresses to hosts that request them","It translates multicast traffic into unicast for each receiver"],1,"In PIM-SM, receivers join the shared tree toward the RP, and sources register with the RP. Flood and prune is PIM dense mode behavior.","Exam topics section 3.0 (multicast)"],["en52",0,3,"Multicast traffic arrives on an interface that is not the router's path back to the source, and the router drops it. Which check caused the drop?",["The IGMP querier election","The TTL scoping check","The PIM DR election","The reverse path forwarding (RPF) check"],3,"RPF accepts multicast only on the interface the router would use to reach the source (or RP), which prevents loops. RPF failures are a common multicast troubleshooting issue.","Exam topics section 3.0 (multicast)"],["en53",0,3,"Which multicast model lets a receiver ask for traffic from a specific source, removing the need for an RP?",["Any-source multicast with IGMPv2","PIM dense mode with IGMPv1","Bidirectional PIM with a phantom rendezvous point","Source-specific multicast (SSM) with IGMPv3"],3,"SSM uses IGMPv3 (S,G) joins so routers build a source tree directly, with no RP. IGMPv2 cannot specify a source. SSM uses the 232.0.0.0/8 range by default.","Exam topics section 3.0 (multicast)"],["en54",0,4,"An engineer needs debug output only for traffic from host 10.1.1.50 on a busy production router. What is the safest approach?",["Enable debug all and filter the log file afterward","Use a conditional debug limited to that host or interface","Enable debug ip packet with no ACL and send the output to the console","Reload the router with debugging enabled at startup"],1,"Conditional debugging (debug condition, or an ACL on debug ip packet) limits output so the CPU is not overwhelmed. Unfiltered debugs on a busy router can cause an outage.","Exam topics section 4.0 (diagnose network problems)"],["en55",0,4,"A traceroute from R1 to 192.0.2.10 shows asterisks at hop 4, but later hops respond normally. What is the most likely explanation?",["That device does not send ICMP time-exceeded replies, or rate-limits them","The destination is unreachable because a loop exists at hop 4","The hop-4 device is silently dropping all traffic headed to the destination","The traceroute TTL was set too low to reach hop 5"],0,"If later hops answer, traffic passes hop 4; that device is just not replying to expired probes. A real blocking point would stop all later replies.","Exam topics section 4.0 (diagnose network problems)"],["en56",0,4,"Which SNMP version adds authentication and encryption for manager-to-agent traffic?",["SNMPv2c with a read-only community string","SNMPv1 with a long community string","SNMPv3 with the authPriv security level","SNMPv2c with traps instead of polling"],2,"SNMPv3 authPriv provides both authentication and encryption. v1 and v2c send community strings in clear text.","Exam topics section 4.0 (SNMP and syslog)"],["en57",0,4,"A router is configured with logging trap warnings. Which messages are sent to the syslog server?",["Severity level 4 only","Severity levels 0 through 4","Severity levels 4 through 7","Severity levels 5 through 7"],1,"The trap level sends the chosen severity and everything more severe (lower number): 0 emergencies through 4 warnings.","Exam topics section 4.0 (SNMP and syslog)"],["en58",0,4,"The team wants to see which applications and hosts use the most WAN bandwidth, with custom key fields. What should be configured on the router?",["SPAN copying the WAN interface to a local analyzer port","IP SLA ICMP echo probes to the WAN next hop","SNMP interface counters polled every five minutes by the NMS","Flexible NetFlow with a flow record, exporter and monitor"],3,"Flexible NetFlow builds per-flow records with the key and non-key fields you choose and exports them to a collector. SNMP counters give totals, not per-flow detail.","Exam topics section 4.0 (NetFlow)"],["en59",0,4,"An IDS appliance is in the data center, three routed hops away from the switch whose port must be monitored. Which feature copies the traffic to it?",["Local SPAN, which copies to another port on the same switch","RSPAN, which carries the mirrored traffic in a dedicated VLAN across Layer 3 hops","ERSPAN, which carries the mirrored traffic in GRE across routed hops","Port security, which forwards unknown frames to a monitor port"],2,"ERSPAN uses GRE so mirrored traffic can cross routed hops. RSPAN uses a dedicated VLAN, so it works only across Layer 2 trunks.","Exam topics section 4.0 (SPAN/RSPAN/ERSPAN)"],["en60",0,4,"A branch has a primary and a backup internet link. The team wants the static default route to be withdrawn if the ISP's DNS server stops responding, even while the link stays up. What should they use?",["An IP SLA ICMP echo probe tracked by the static route","A floating static route with an administrative distance of 250","SNMP traps that alert the NOC when the link goes down","A NetFlow exporter that sends flow records to the ISP"],0,"IP SLA plus object tracking removes the static route when the probe fails, even if the interface is up. A floating static route only takes over when the primary route disappears.","Exam topics section 4.0 (IP SLA)"],["en61",0,4,"Which Catalyst Center (formerly DNA Center) feature helps an engineer find the root cause of a user's poor experience over the past day?",["Assurance, which correlates device, client and app health over time","Plug and Play, which onboards new devices with day-0 configuration","Software image management, which checks devices against golden images","Template editor, which pushes configuration to groups of devices"],0,"Assurance collects telemetry and shows health scores, issues and suggested fixes, including AI-driven insights. PnP, SWIM and templates are provisioning tools.","Exam topics section 4.0 (Catalyst Center workflows)"],["en62",0,4,"An engineer wants to change the hostname of a router programmatically using a YANG model over SSH (port 830). Which protocol should be used?",["RESTCONF","SNMPv3","NETCONF","gNMI over HTTP/1.1"],2,"NETCONF uses SSH (port 830) and XML-encoded YANG data. RESTCONF uses HTTPS with JSON or XML.","Exam topics section 4.0 (NETCONF and RESTCONF)"],["en63",0,5,"An engineer wants console and VTY logins to use the local user database. Which configuration applies to the VTY lines?",["login, with a password set under the line","login local, with usernames defined by username commands","transport input none, with a local username","exec-timeout 0 0, with an enable secret"],1,"login local checks the local username database. Plain login only uses the line password, and transport input none blocks remote access entirely.","Exam topics section 5.0 (device access control)"],["en64",0,5,"The team wants to authorize each command an administrator enters on routers and log it centrally. Which AAA protocol is the best choice?",["RADIUS, because it encrypts the whole packet body","RADIUS, because it runs over TCP for reliability and logs each command centrally","TACACS+, because it combines authentication and authorization","TACACS+, because it separates authorization and can authorize each command"],3,"TACACS+ uses TCP 49, encrypts the whole body and separates authentication, authorization and accounting, which enables per-command authorization. RADIUS combines authentication and authorization and encrypts only the password.","Exam topics section 5.0 (AAA)"],["en65",0,5,"A router is configured with aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ local. The TACACS+ server is unreachable. What happens when an admin logs in?",["The login is denied because TACACS+ is listed first","The router allows access with no password","The router tries RADIUS before the local database","The router falls back to the local user database"],3,"The method list is tried in order, and the next method is used only when the previous one returns an error, such as an unreachable server. A reject from TACACS+ would not fall back.","Exam topics section 5.0 (AAA)"],["en66",0,5,"Which port-based network access control method authenticates a user with credentials through a supplicant and a RADIUS server?",["MAC Authentication Bypass","802.1X","WebAuth with a guest portal","Port security with sticky MAC"],1,"802.1X uses a supplicant, the switch as authenticator and a RADIUS server. MAB authenticates by MAC address for devices without a supplicant.","Exam topics section 5.0 (network access control)"],["en67",0,5,"A printer has no 802.1X supplicant but must be placed in the correct VLAN automatically by ISE. Which method should the switch use?",["MAC Authentication Bypass (MAB)","EAP-TLS with a device certificate","Local WebAuth on the switch","Port security with a maximum of one MAC"],0,"MAB sends the device's MAC address to RADIUS as its identity, so ISE can authorize it. EAP-TLS needs a supplicant, which the printer lacks.","Exam topics section 5.0 (network access control)"],["en68",0,5,"An extended ACL must block Telnet from 10.1.1.0/24 to server 10.2.2.10 but allow everything else. Which entry order is correct?",["permit ip any any, then deny tcp 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.2.2.10 eq 23","deny tcp host 10.2.2.10 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 eq 23, then permit ip any any","deny tcp 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.2.2.10 eq 23, then permit ip any any","deny ip 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.2.2.10, then permit tcp any any eq 23"],2,"ACLs are read top down and stop at the first match, so the specific deny must come before the permit. The source is the client subnet and the destination port is 23 on the server.","Exam topics section 5.0 (ACLs)"],["en69",0,5,"Where should an extended ACL generally be placed?",["As close to the destination of the traffic as possible","As close to the source of the traffic as possible","Only on the router's loopback interface","Only on VTY lines"],1,"Extended ACLs match source, destination and ports, so placing them near the source drops unwanted traffic early. Standard ACLs, which match only the source, go near the destination.","Exam topics section 5.0 (ACLs)"],["en70",0,5,"A router's CPU spikes because of a flood of ICMP and SSH attempts aimed at the router itself. Which feature limits that traffic to the control plane?",["Unicast Reverse Path Forwarding","An interface ACL on the outbound direction","Storm control on the switch access ports","Control Plane Policing (CoPP)"],3,"CoPP applies a QoS policy to traffic destined to the route processor, rate-limiting it to protect the CPU. uRPF checks source addresses for spoofing, and storm control limits broadcasts on switch ports.","Exam topics section 5.0 (CoPP)"],["en71",0,5,"Which CoPP design is recommended?",["Police all control plane traffic to the same low rate","Drop all ICMP and SNMP traffic sent to the router","Classify traffic, allow routing protocols more and police the rest tightly","Apply one policy outbound on every physical interface instead of the control plane"],2,"CoPP classes let critical traffic such as BGP and OSPF through while limiting management and unknown traffic. A single low limit can drop routing updates and cause instability.","Exam topics section 5.0 (CoPP)"],["en72",0,5,"A script calls a controller's REST API. Which practice best protects the API credentials and data in transit?",["Use HTTPS with token authentication and keep tokens out of the code","Use HTTP with basic authentication and store the password in the script","Use HTTPS but put the API key in the URL query string","Use HTTP but restrict the API to the management VLAN"],0,"HTTPS protects data in transit, and tokens kept in a vault or environment variable avoid hard-coded secrets. Query strings are logged by proxies and servers.","Exam topics section 5.0 (REST API security)"],["en73",0,5,"How does a next-generation firewall differ from a traditional stateful firewall?",["It adds application awareness, IPS and threat intelligence to stateful inspection","It filters only on source and destination ports without tracking any session state","It replaces routing, so the network needs no routers","It inspects only Layer 2 frames and cannot see IP headers"],0,"An NGFW identifies applications and users and adds IPS and malware protection. A traditional stateful firewall tracks connections but decides mostly on addresses and ports.","Exam topics section 5.0 (security design components)"],["en74",0,5,"A user downloads a file that later turns out to be malicious. Which component can retroactively flag the file and quarantine it on every endpoint where it exists?",["A stateful firewall at the internet edge","An IPsec VPN concentrator","An endpoint security product with retrospective analysis, such as Cisco Secure Endpoint","802.1X port authentication on the switch"],2,"Endpoint protection with retrospective security tracks file dispositions and can act after a verdict changes. Firewalls and 802.1X do not track files on endpoints.","Exam topics section 5.0 (security design components)"],["en75",0,5,"How does Cisco TrustSec enforce policy?",["It builds a separate VLAN for every user and routes between them","It tags traffic with a scalable group tag (SGT) and enforces permissions with SGACLs based on group, not IP address","It encrypts every packet with IPsec between the endpoint and the firewall","It blocks unknown MAC addresses with port security"],1,"TrustSec classifies users and devices into groups (SGTs) and enforces group-to-group SGACLs, so policy does not depend on IP addressing.","Exam topics section 5.0 (TrustSec)"],["en76",0,5,"A bank needs to encrypt traffic on the link between two switches at Layer 2, hop by hop. Which technology fits?",["IPsec tunnel mode","TLS 1.3","SSH version 2","MACsec (802.1AE)"],3,"MACsec encrypts Ethernet frames on each link at line rate. IPsec and TLS operate at Layer 3 and above.","Exam topics section 5.0 (MACsec)"],["en77",0,5,"Which switch feature blocks rogue DHCP servers on access ports?",["Dynamic ARP inspection on every trunk port","IP source guard on the DHCP server port","Storm control on the access ports","DHCP snooping, with only uplinks to the real server marked trusted"],3,"DHCP snooping drops DHCP server messages (offers and acks) on untrusted ports. DAI and IP source guard rely on the snooping binding table but do not stop rogue servers.","Exam topics section 5.0 (infrastructure security)"],["en78",0,5,"A guest must accept an acceptable use policy in a browser before getting network access, with no supplicant needed. Which method is this?",["802.1X with PEAP","Web authentication (WebAuth) with a captive portal","MAC Authentication Bypass","MACsec with a pre-shared key"],1,"WebAuth redirects the user's browser to a portal to log in or accept terms. 802.1X needs a supplicant, and MAB uses the MAC address without user interaction.","Exam topics section 5.0 (network access control)"],["en79",0,5,"Why should an engineer configure transport input ssh on the VTY lines?",["To reject Telnet, which sends credentials in clear text","To allow Telnet and SSH at the same time","To disable console access for remote users","To force the use of SNMPv3 for management"],0,"transport input ssh allows only SSH on the VTY lines. SSH also needs a hostname, domain name and RSA key pair.","Exam topics section 5.0 (device access control)"],["en80",0,5,"Which statement correctly describes the difference between RADIUS and TACACS+ transport?",["RADIUS uses TCP port 49, and TACACS+ uses UDP 1812","Both use UDP, but only TACACS+ encrypts the password","RADIUS uses UDP (1812/1813), and TACACS+ uses TCP port 49","Both use TCP, but only RADIUS encrypts the whole payload"],2,"RADIUS runs over UDP and encrypts only the password field; TACACS+ runs over TCP 49 and encrypts the whole body.","Exam topics section 5.0 (AAA)"],["en81",0,6,"A Python script receives a JSON response and needs the value \"up\" from {\"interface\": {\"name\": \"Gi1\", \"status\": \"up\"}}. The response is loaded into the variable data. Which expression returns it?",["data.interface.status()","data[\"interface\"][\"status\"]","data[0][1]","data[\"status\"][\"interface\"]"],1,"json.loads returns nested dictionaries, so you index by key from the outside in. Dictionaries are not indexed by position.","Exam topics section 6.0 (Python and JSON)"],["en82",0,6,"Which JSON snippet is valid?",["{'vlans': [10, 20], 'enabled': True}","{\"vlans\": (10, 20), \"enabled\": true}","{vlans: [10, 20], enabled: true}","{\"vlans\": [10, 20], \"enabled\": true}"],3,"JSON requires double-quoted keys and strings, square brackets for arrays and lowercase true/false. Single quotes and True are Python syntax, not JSON.","Exam topics section 6.0 (JSON)"],["en83",0,6,"What does a YANG model define?",["The transport and port a management protocol uses","The encryption used between a controller and its devices","The structure and data types of configuration and operational data that a device exposes","The Python library used to send API requests"],2,"YANG is a data modeling language: containers, lists, leaves and their types. NETCONF and RESTCONF carry data that follows YANG models.","Exam topics section 6.0 (YANG)"],["en84",0,6,"A RESTCONF request to update an interface returns HTTP 401. What does this indicate?",["The request lacked valid authentication credentials","The resource was created successfully","The URL path does not exist on the device","The server had an internal error"],0,"401 Unauthorized means authentication is missing or invalid. 201 is Created, 404 is Not Found and 500 is an internal server error.","Exam topics section 6.0 (REST response codes)"],["en85",0,6,"Which HTTP method should a script use to retrieve the device inventory from the Catalyst Center API without changing anything?",["GET","POST","PUT","DELETE"],0,"GET reads a resource without side effects. POST creates, PUT replaces and DELETE removes.","Exam topics section 6.0 (Catalyst Center APIs)"],["en86",0,6,"Before calling other Catalyst Center Intent API endpoints, what must a script usually do first?",["Send a GET to the device list with no headers","Open an SSH session to the controller on port 830","Send a POST to the authentication endpoint with basic credentials to obtain a token","Register the script's MAC address in ISE"],2,"The script POSTs to the token endpoint and then sends the token in the X-Auth-Token header on later calls.","Exam topics section 6.0 (Catalyst Center APIs)"],["en87",0,6,"An engineer needs a script to pull the list of WAN edge devices and their tunnel health across all branches. Which API should the script target?",["The SD-WAN Validator (formerly vBond) CLI","The SD-WAN Manager (formerly vManage) REST API","The WAN Edge local NETCONF interface on each branch","The SNMP MIB on the SD-WAN Controller"],1,"SD-WAN Manager is the central management point and exposes REST APIs for inventory, monitoring and configuration across the fabric. Querying each edge separately does not scale.","Exam topics section 6.0 (SD-WAN Manager APIs)"],["en88",0,6,"An engineer wants a router to automatically save a copy of its running configuration to a TFTP server whenever someone changes it, using only on-box features. What should be used?",["An Ansible playbook run from a cron job on a server","A NetFlow monitor that exports configuration records","An IP SLA probe that tracks the TFTP server","An EEM applet that triggers on the configuration change syslog message"],3,"Embedded Event Manager runs on the device and can react to syslog patterns such as the configuration change message, then run CLI actions. Ansible runs off-box.","Exam topics section 6.0 (EEM)"],["en89",0,6,"Which configuration management tool is agentless and connects to network devices over SSH using YAML playbooks?",["Puppet","Chef","SaltStack with minions","Ansible"],3,"Ansible is agentless and uses SSH (or APIs) with YAML playbooks. Puppet and Chef traditionally use agents that pull from a master server, and Salt uses minions.","Exam topics section 6.0 (orchestration tools)"],["en90",0,6,"What is a key difference between agent-based and agentless orchestration?",["Agent-based tools use only SSH, while agentless tools need a local daemon","Agent-based tools install software on each managed node, while agentless tools use existing access such as SSH or APIs","Agentless tools cannot manage network devices, only servers","Agent-based tools never use a central server for configuration"],1,"Agents run on the managed node and usually pull configuration from a server. Agentless tools push over existing protocols, which suits network devices where agents may not be installable.","Exam topics section 6.0 (orchestration tools)"],["en91",0,6,"A Python script should retry a REST call only if the device returns status 503. Which construct handles this cleanly?",["A while loop that checks response.status_code == 503 and stops after a set number of attempts","A for loop over the JSON keys of the response body","A single if statement that exits when the status is 200","A try block that catches KeyError from the dictionary"],0,"A bounded loop that checks the status code retries safely without looping forever. KeyError handling is about missing dictionary keys, not HTTP status.","Exam topics section 6.0 (Python)"],["en92",0,6,"How can AI-driven analytics in Catalyst Center improve operations compared with fixed static thresholds?",["It removes the need to collect any telemetry from network devices","It guarantees that no configuration change can cause an outage","It learns a baseline of normal behavior for each site and flags anomalies that deviate from it","It replaces routing protocols with machine learning path selection"],2,"AI/ML-based assurance builds dynamic baselines and highlights deviations and likely root causes, reducing noise from static thresholds. It still depends on telemetry and does not replace routing.","Exam topics section 6.0 (AI in network operations)"],["en93",0,6,"A team plans to let an AI assistant propose and push configuration changes. Which control is most important?",["Giving the assistant full admin access so it can fix issues quickly","Human approval and least-privilege access, with changes logged and reversible","Disabling logging so the assistant's changes do not fill syslog","Allowing the assistant to change production without a change window"],1,"AI output can be wrong, so changes need review, scoped permissions, audit logs and rollback. Unrestricted access removes the safety net.","Exam topics section 6.0 (AI in network operations)"]]);
